hw/m68k/mcf_intc: implement writes to INTFRCH/INTFRCL

INTFRCH (+0x10) and INTFRCL (+0x14) are the software interrupt-force registers.
Both offsets were already readable and `s->ifr` already took part in
arbitration, but neither had a write case: writes fell through to the default
label, were logged as a bad offset and discarded. No bit could ever be set,
which leaves the feature inert while looking half-present — a driver reads back
0 and has no other signal.

Any driver that defers work by forcing a lower-priority source silently loses
that work; a ColdFire firmware that hands off from a high-IPL receive ISR to a
parser at a lower IPL simply never runs the parser. Semantics are plain
read/write level per MCF54455RM 17.3.6 — software sets a bit to force the
source and clears it to release, with no write-1-to-clear — so the two cases
mirror the INTFRCH/INTFRCL reads above them and fall through to the existing
mcf_intc_update().

diff --git a/hw/m68k/mcf_intc.c b/hw/m68k/mcf_intc.c
--- a/hw/m68k/mcf_intc.c
+++ b/hw/m68k/mcf_intc.c
@@ -124,6 +124,23 @@
     case 0x0c:
         s->imr = (s->imr & 0xffffffff00000000ull) | (uint32_t)val;
         break;
+    /*
+     * INTFRCH / INTFRCL -- software interrupt force. Both offsets were already
+     * READABLE and s->ifr already participates in arbitration
+     * (active = (ipr | ifr) & enabled & ~imr), but writes fell through to the
+     * default case and were discarded as a guest error, so nothing could ever
+     * set a bit. Any driver that hands work to a lower-priority level by forcing
+     * its source silently loses it.
+     *
+     * Read/write level semantics, per MCF54455RM 17.3.6: software sets a bit to
+     * force the source and clears it to release. No write-1-to-clear.
+     */
+    case 0x10:
+        s->ifr = (s->ifr & 0xffffffffull) | ((uint64_t)val << 32);
+        break;
+    case 0x14:
+        s->ifr = (s->ifr & 0xffffffff00000000ull) | (uint32_t)val;
+        break;
     case 0x1c:
         if (val & 0x40) {
             s->imr = ~0ull;
